1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,100
Imagine surviving an atomic bomb.

2
00:00:03,100 --> 00:00:05,400
Now imagine getting on a train,

3
00:00:05,400 --> 00:00:07,200
traveling to another city…

4
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:09,700
and getting hit by a second one.

5
00:00:09,700 --> 00:00:11,200
Three days later.

6
00:00:11,200 --> 00:00:12,700
This actually happened.

7
00:00:12,700 --> 00:00:15,400
His name was Tsutomu Yamaguchi.

8
00:00:15,400 --> 00:00:19,000
In 1945, Tsutomu was a 29-year-old engineer

9
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:21,400
who designed oil tankers for Mitsubishi

10
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:24,000
in his hometown of Nagasaki.

11
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:29,400
That summer, his company sent him on a three-month business trip to Hiroshima.

12
00:00:29,400 --> 00:00:33,900
On August 6th, it was his last day there.

13
00:00:33,900 --> 00:00:38,100
Tomorrow, he'd finally go home to his wife and baby son.

14
00:00:38,100 --> 00:00:40,400
That morning, he was walking to the shipyard

15
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:43,400
when he heard a plane overhead.

16
00:00:43,400 --> 00:00:47,900
He looked up and saw something drifting down on a parachute.

17
00:00:47,900 --> 00:00:48,700
Then,

18
00:00:48,700 --> 00:00:51,600
a flash brighter than the sun.

19
00:00:51,600 --> 00:00:54,600
He was about three kilometers from the blast.

20
00:00:54,600 --> 00:00:59,000
The shockwave threw him to the ground.

21
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:01,200
His eardrums burst.

22
00:01:01,200 --> 00:01:05,400
The left side of his upper body was badly burned.

23
00:01:05,400 --> 00:01:09,600
When he looked up, a giant mushroom cloud was rising over the city.

24
00:01:09,600 --> 00:01:12,200
He spent the night in an air-raid shelter.

25
00:01:12,200 --> 00:01:15,200
The next morning, he made his way to the station,

26
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:17,500
found one of the few trains still running,

27
00:01:17,500 --> 00:01:19,900
and rode it home to Nagasaki.

28
00:01:19,900 --> 00:01:25,000
Wrapped in bandages, exhausted, he had survived the unthinkable.

29
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:27,300
Surely, the worst was over.

30
00:01:27,300 --> 00:01:31,600
On August 9th, he insisted on going back to work.

31
00:01:31,600 --> 00:01:35,200
He was telling his supervisor what he'd seen.

32
00:01:35,200 --> 00:01:38,500
One bomb destroying an entire city.

33
00:01:38,500 --> 00:01:40,700
His boss didn't believe him.

34
00:01:40,700 --> 00:01:43,200
"That's impossible," he said.

35
00:01:43,200 --> 00:01:45,700
"One bomb can't do that."

36
00:01:45,700 --> 00:01:47,900
And at that exact moment,

37
00:01:47,900 --> 00:01:52,400
the room filled with the same blinding white light.

38
00:01:52,400 --> 00:01:58,100
Once again, Tsutomu was about three kilometers from ground zero.

39
00:01:58,100 --> 00:02:01,000
Somehow, he survived again.

40
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:06,900
His bandages were torn off, and he fell sick with fever for over a week.

41
00:02:06,900 --> 00:02:10,500
But his wife and son had also survived.

42
00:02:10,500 --> 00:02:15,100
And Tsutomu went on to live a long life.

43
00:02:15,100 --> 00:02:20,200
He had more children, worked as a teacher and an engineer again.

44
00:02:20,200 --> 00:02:26,900
And in 2009, the Japanese government officially recognized him as a survivor of both bombings,

45
00:02:26,900 --> 00:02:30,800
the only person ever formally given that status.

46
00:02:30,800 --> 00:02:34,800
In his later years, he traveled and spoke out against nuclear weapons,

47
00:02:34,800 --> 00:02:37,900
so no one would ever experience what he did.

48
00:02:37,900 --> 00:02:39,300
Twice.

49
00:02:39,300 --> 00:02:44,300
Tsutomu Yamaguchi died in 2010 at the age of 93.

50
00:02:44,300 --> 00:02:47,600
Some people call him the unluckiest man in history.

51
00:02:47,600 --> 00:02:49,700
Others call him the luckiest.

52
00:02:49,700 --> 00:02:51,200
What do you think?

53
00:02:51,200 --> 00:02:56,100
Tell me in the comments and subscribe for more stories that sound too crazy to be true.

